---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CognitiveDiagram from "../../components/CognitiveDiagram.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Cognitive Manufacturing"
  description="ARMS explores Cognitive Manufacturing to rethink manufacturing workflows for people and computers, connecting engineering knowledge and computational tools from design to inspection."
  current="/research/cognitive-manufacturing/"
>
  <section class="section detail-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research / Cognitive Manufacturing</p>
    <h1>Rethinking the manufacturing workflow.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How can we accelerate manufacturing with engineering information that
      people can understand and computers can use?
    </p>
    <p>
      Traditional manufacturing workflows rely on 2D working drawings and the
      expertise of people who interpret them. Engineering intent is repeatedly
      translated into decisions for design, process planning, production, and
      inspection. These handoffs can take time and make information difficult to
      carry consistently from one activity to the next.
    </p>
    <p>
      At ARMS, we are exploring Cognitive Manufacturing to rethink this
      workflow. Our vision is a shared engineering definition that serves both
      people and computers: clear views and explanations for engineers,
      connected information that software can interpret and use. By bringing
      engineering knowledge and computational tools together, we aim to reduce
      repeated interpretation, accelerate the path from design to production,
      and help people make informed decisions throughout the manufacturing
      lifecycle.
    </p>
    <CognitiveDiagram />
  </section>
  <section class="section soft">
    <p class="eyebrow">Two complementary research pillars</p>
    <h2>Engineering meaning. Intelligent collaboration.</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <h3>Semantic Manufacturing</h3>
        <p>
          How can we make engineering information meaningful and usable across
          tools and activities? We explore ways to connect design intent,
          requirements, and manufacturing knowledge, with human-readable views
          and computer-processable information grounded in the same engineering
          definition.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Agentic Manufacturing</h3>
        <p>
          How can software agents help with engineering work? We explore how
          agents can reason, use engineering tools, and collaborate with people
          to move work forward, from interpreting a task to proposing actions
          and checking outcomes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section closing">
    <p class="eyebrow">An emerging research direction</p>
    <h2>Explore the future of manufacturing with us.</h2>
    <p>
      This emerging research direction offers opportunities to connect
      mechanical engineering with software development, CAD/CAM, and
      experimental manufacturing. Potential projects include representing
      engineering knowledge, developing software agents for engineering, and
      connecting design with manufacturing and inspection.
    </p>
    <p>
      We welcome curious students who enjoy building, experimenting, and working
      across disciplines, as well as collaborators with manufacturing challenges
      to explore.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="/open-positions/">
        Student inquiry information →
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="mailto:msoshi@ucdavis.edu">
        Discuss collaboration
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .detail-intro > :global(.cognitive-diagram) {
    margin-top: 36px;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
</style>
